WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.597
For this module, the sample database intentionally contains messy data so that we can practice realistic cleaning

00:00:08.597 --> 00:00:16.897
decisions. The customers table includes missing values, blank text, inconsistent names, different phone formats,

00:00:16.897 --> 00:00:21.992
inconsistent email formatting, and possible duplicate identities.

00:00:21.992 --> 00:00:29.233
The products table includes inconsistent category labels, invalid prices, and stock problems.

00:00:29.683 --> 00:00:33.770
The messy customers table acts more like a staging table.

00:00:33.770 --> 00:00:40.985
It contains mixed date formats, an invalid date, empty values, and a duplicate record.

00:00:40.985 --> 00:00:45.035
We will use it later to build a more complete cleaning pipeline.

00:00:45.035 --> 00:00:49.095
The orders table gives us additional relational context.

00:00:49.095 --> 00:00:54.505
As you work through the examples, do not think of these problems as unusual.

00:00:54.505 --> 00:00:59.930
Real operational databases often contain exactly this kind of variation,

00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:07.177
especially when information comes from multiple systems, manual entry, spreadsheets, or older applications.
